# #1637 (EigenScript#1647): `true`/`false` are boolean literals -- keywords in
# the C lexer's TOK_IS..TOK_LOCAL run, so they are no longer identifiers
# (`true is 5` is now a parse error on both sides: test/run.sh reject_one) but
# still serve as dot keys. This file used to pin the opposite (they were plain
# names, `print of true` printed the bound 5); the pin moved the language.
print of true
print of false
print of [true, false, not true, not false]
print of (type of true)
print of (true == true)
print of (true != false)
print of (true and 5)
print of (false or "x")
x is false
if not x:
    print of "falsy"
if true:
    print of "truthy"
d is {"a": 1}
d.true is 3
d.false is 4
print of d.true
print of d.false
print of d
define flip(b) as:
    return not b
print of (flip of true)
print of (flip of false)
local truthy is 1
print of truthy
print of f"{true}-{false}"
print of (str of true)
